                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "5/10. \n\nWas excited to play this game, only to be let down :(\n\nThis is an old school retro platformer. While the controls feel solid a majority of the time, they can also be finnicky. The wallrun doesnt work half the time, and sometimes you will want to pull up onto a ledge and it will have you jump off instead. Combat isn't great as guys take many kills and you die in one hit. All of the levels look the same and the environments are boring, blocking, and look copy and pasted with just a few color/background changes. There is no story. There is like 3 enemies total. You do the same thing in each environment. You hunt down keys, go into a building, and unlock a door. It gets super repetitive. Then there's the biggest problem, the map design. Each level has a few branching paths, and then some of those have branching paths. But since there is no map whatsoever and all the ways look the same it is hard to remember which way you came or you need to go. There are no markers or directions at all. The other biggest problem with the design is you will go super far one path, having beaten a bunch of hard parts, then you find at the end you need a key. So you have to run all the way back. There's nothing you can do, since no markers or map you have to trial and error hoping you found the right way. Even if you do, you will find a key, but you have to go all the way back again to use it in another area. This is just artificially inflating game length and is lazy design that makes the game boring. I would then get back and since it is hard to tell where you've been I would go back one of the paths I had come, but theres no way of knowing till you get back to start, then you have to retrace your steps again. So much of my time in the game was wasted retracing steps, going wrong way, etc. This saps all the fun from the game and makes it a chore. Finally I took a day off and was at a part with 3 paths, went 1 way, didn't have a key, had no idea which way I had come from the day before, went back one way, and finally decided it wasn't worth it and gave up. \n\nThe sad thing is, even with the finnicky control and combat, the game could be fun. But the terrible map design, lack of direction, forced backtracking and trial and error on paths, and repetitiveness of each area, just make this game an amateur effort. I just think of what could have been......",
